{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TptmQZLCGpRxKNZfooRGQb","type":"tool_result","content":"========== blockingFindings ==========\n{\n  \"id\": \"F-EPIC-001\",\n  \"severity\": \"major\",\n  \"title\": \"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a thread with no durable file\",\n  \"evidence\": \"src/domains/threads/index.ts:73 guards only with existsSync(input.filePath); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database that vanishes on close, so newThread({ filePath: '' }) succeeds and inserts a registry row for filePath ''. Independently reproduced on the built CLI against a clean default registry: `threads new-thread --file-path ''` \\u2192 { ok: true, value: { threadId: 'th_bd276835dfc0a9a2', filePath: '' } }; `threads resolve` returned the row; `intake-stream list-events --thread-id th_bd276835dfc0a9a2` \\u2192 { errorClass: 'caller_error', code: 'thread_not_found', reason: 'no thread file exists at ' }. This violates the no-row-without-file invariant the code itself documents as 'absolute' (threads/index.ts:66-69) and the Story 1 boundary note in epic.md, and breaks AC-1.1 (no file created), AC-1.3 (no file to read the id from), and AC-1.4 (row points at nothing). Corroborating evidence the hazard was known: the CLI guard comment at src/cli/index.ts:99-100 names the empty-path/temp-database failure mode, but the guard checks only `flags.filePath === undefined`, so an empty string passes; and the intake envelope's ThreadRefSchema already requires NonEmptyString for filePath (validate.ts:38) while newThread accepts any string. No test covers the empty-path case on SDK or CLI. Fix is small and contract-aligned: reject empty (or whitespace-only) filePath in newThread with a caller_error before any storage touch, plus SDK and CLI tests.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/index.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/test/threads.test.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [\n    \"AC-1.1\",\n    \"AC-1.3\",\n    \"AC-1.4\",\n    \"TC-1.1\",\n    \"TC-1.6\"\n  ],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n  \"blocking\": true\n}\n----\n========== nonBlockingFindings ==========\n{\n  \"id\": \"NB-1\",\n  \"severity\": \"minor\",\n  \"title\": \"Empty-string payload text/content accepted despite epic Flow 4's 'missing or empty required field' rule\",\n  \"evidence\": \"Epic Flow 4 lists 'a missing or empty required field: idempotency key, actor, harness, or the payload fields its kind requires' as invalid. validate.ts types text and tool_result.content as Schema.String (validate.ts:55, 63), so { text: '' } and empty tool_result content pass validation, while empty toolCallId/toolName/actor/harness/idempotencyKey are rejected via NonEmptyString. The tech design's interfaces typed these as plain string, so the drift originates between epic and design; the implementation followed the design. Whether an empty tool result (a real occurrence) should be a rejection is a product call.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/validate.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [\n    \"AC-4.2\",\n    \"AC-4.4\"\n  ],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n  \"blocking\": false\n}\n----\n{\n  \"id\": \"NB-2\",\n  \"severity\": \"minor\",\n  \"title\": \"CLI parses flags with strict:false, silently ignoring unknown or misspelled flags\",\n  \"evidence\": \"src/cli/index.ts:53-64 calls parseArgs with strict: false, so `lhc threads new-thread --file-path p --titel x` creates the thread with no title and no error. This is the silent-drop failure mode the epic's boundary philosophy ('Nothing is silently dropped') exists to prevent, though the closed-contract rule formally covers the event envelope, not argv. A strict: true parse with a structured usage error would align the CLI boundary with the SDK boundary's strictness.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [\n    \"AC-4.7\"\n  ],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n  \"blocking\": false\n}\n----\n{\n  \"id\": \"F-EPIC-002\",\n  \"severity\": \"minor\",\n  \"title\": \"Read-back surfaces skip thread-reference validation at both SDK and CLI layers\",\n  \"evidence\": \"Merged from epic-reviewer-1 NB-3 and epic-reviewer-2 F-EPIC-002 \\u2014 the same gap at two layers. SDK: messageEvents and listEvents validate the thread reference against the closed ThreadRef schema (pipeline.ts:92, 267), but listMessages, listTurns, and both listQueuedWork operations pass the reference straight to resolveThreadRef, so unknown fields or an empty filePath are tolerated. CLI: `messages list`, `messages list-queued-work`, `turns list`, and `turns list-queued-work` (cli/index.ts:176-183) lack the --thread-id/--file-path missing-flag guard the intake commands have, and threadRefFrom defaults a missing path to '' (cli/index.ts:79); verified this pass that `node dist/cli.js messages list` returns thread_not_found for an empty path instead of a usage error. Behavior remains fail-closed; the inconsistency is in which error the caller gets and whether unknown ref fields are named.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/src/domains/messages/index.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/src/domains/turns/index.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [\n    \"AC-1.6\",\n    \"AC-4.7\"\n  ],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n  \"blocking\": false\n}\n----\n{\n  \"id\": \"NB-4\",\n  \"severity\": \"observation\",\n  \"title\": \"Mutable test seams exported from the production pipeline module\",\n  \"evidence\": \"setIntakeWalkHook and setIntakeClock (pipeline.ts:23, 34) are module-global mutable state in production code, reachable by any importer of the internal module path even though the boundary check confines them to test/fixtures and the package export map exposes only the SDK root. Unset they are inert (real wall clock when unset; explicit clock argument wins). The walk hook is design-sanctioned by the test plan's architecture-risk row; the clock setter is an addition beyond the design's context-injected clock. Consider relocating behind a single test-only entry or documenting them in the design's seam inventory when Epic 02 extends the context.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/domains/intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/test/fixtures/intake-seam.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n  \"blocking\": false\n}\n----\n{\n  \"id\": \"NB-5\",\n  \"severity\": \"observation\",\n  \"title\": \"Dead stub helper and stale dist artifacts left from earlier phases\",\n  \"evidence\": \"shared/errors.ts:36 notImplemented() has no callers since the Story 0 CLI stubs were replaced (verified: only the definition remains in src/). dist/ retains compiled output from the pre-epic reference layout (dist/commands/, dist/thread-events/, dist/token-counting/ confirmed present) because tsc does not clean; the package is private so nothing ships, but a clean step would prevent confusion about what the production build contains.\",\n  \"affectedFiles\": [\n    \"packages/lhc/src/shared/errors.ts\",\n    \"packages/lhc/dist\"\n  ],\n  \"requirementIds\": [],\n  \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n  \"blocking\": false\n}\n----","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36778e1e-3bb5-45c6-8164-3cc17eb2f31a","uuid":"9045c26a-0613-4c5a-aeb0-1a379157856c","timestamp":"2026-06-10T15:40:23.340Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"========== blockingFindings ==========\n{\n  \"id\": \"F-EPIC-001\",\n  \"severity\": \"major\",\n  \"title\": \"Thread creation accepts an empty path and registers a thread with no durable file\",\n  \"evidence\": \"src/domains/threads/index.ts:73 guards only with existsSync(input.filePath); node:sqlite's DatabaseSync('') opens a temporary database that vanishes on close, so newThread({ filePath: